  "briefing": {
    "headline": "The model predicts the two home days would cut departures over the next 12 months from about 751 to about 495 of 5,000 staff (roughly 34% fewer, or about 256 people retained), with overall sentiment clearly positive (+1.19 on a -2 to +2 scale).",
    "most_at_risk": "No group reacts badly; caregivers (-40% leaving) and long commuters (-38%) gain the most because each home day spares them roughly two to three hours of travel and eases school and childcare logistics, while short commuters gain least (-28%) and some say they will still come in most days.",
    "risks": [
      "The policy says promotion and reviews won't change, but if managers quietly favour people they see in the office, the caregivers and long commuters who benefit most could end up disadvantaged, which would erode trust and reverse the retention gain.",
      "Every modelled voice was positive, so the size of the effect is probably optimistic; plan around a smaller benefit, and expect any rollback after announcement to land worse than never offering it.",
      "Fixing Wednesday and Friday for everyone may crowd meetings and collaboration into Monday, Tuesday and Thursday and leave offices half-empty on home days. Friday at home may also be seen internally as a soft long weekend, inviting a backlash from senior leaders."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "Make the 'promotion doesn't change' promise checkable: tell managers that working from home on the set days is neutral in reviews, and track ratings and promotion rates for frequent home-workers against office-goers each cycle.",
      "Announce it as a standing policy with a review date (e.g. six months) and clear success measures such as attrition, delivery and engagement, rather than an open-ended trial that staff fear could be withdrawn.",
      "Give team leads a small amount of flex (e.g. swap which two days are home days, or agree core collaboration hours on office days) so the fixed Wednesday/Friday pattern doesn't overload office days or clash with team rhythms."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, because the direction (fewer people leaving, biggest gains for long commuters and caregivers) is consistent across every group and fits what employees say, but the exact 34% rests on 48 modelled voices that are uniformly positive and should be treated as a rough guide, not a forecast."
  },
